course: dictionary
lesson: 13036
title: Famished: very, very hungry
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Famished» is a strong word for hungry. When you say «I'm famished!» after a long day at school, you mean you are very, very hungry, much more than just a bit peckish.
explain: Use the clues around the word to check. «The famished sailors had been at sea for weeks.» After weeks at sea with little food, the sailors would be starving, so famished can mean starving from lack of food over a long time.
explain: Try swapping in «hungry» to test your meaning. «I'm hungry!» still works, but it is weaker. Famished tells the reader that the hunger is strong.
fact: «Famished» means very, very hungry.
fact: «Famished» is informal, so you often hear it in everyday talk, like «I'm famished!»
fact: In stories, «famished» can mean starving from lack of food over a long time.
fact: «Famished» is a stronger word than hungry, so use it when the hunger is really strong.
exercise: «I'm famished! Can we eat now?» What does famished mean? | *Very, very hungry | Full up after a big dinner | Very tired after school | Bored of waiting
exercise: «The famished sailors had been at sea for weeks with almost no food.» Which phrase is closest in meaning to famished here? | Sleepy after a long voyage | Happy to be home | Tired of the sea | *Starving from lack of food over a long time
exercise: «After the match, the whole team was famished.» Find and copy the word that tells you how the team felt. | *famished | after | team | match
test: «The dog was famished, so it ate its whole bowl in seconds.» Why did the dog eat so fast? | It did not like its dinner | It was cold and wet | *It was very, very hungry | It was full and sleepy
test: Which word is the strongest way to say hungry? | peckish | tired | hungry | *famished
exam: «The travellers were famished after days lost in the desert.» What does famished mean here? | Happy to be found | Lost in the sand | *Starving, with no food for a long time | Very tired from walking
